# Rover 400 HH-R (four-door saloon), 1995-1999.
#
# The HH-R was the 1995 restyle of the Rover 400, and the last development of the R8
# platform: a rounded body with the Rover corporate grille of vertical chrome bars
# and integrated elongated headlamps. It was sold as a four-door saloon and a
# five-door hatchback; the saloon is modelled, as the reference photographs show.
# https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rover_400_/_45
# https://rover200.org.uk/cars/hhr/hhr-history
#
# Dimensions 4.320 x 1.695 x 1.390 m, wheelbase 2.620 m; the published HH-R
# dimension box, which gives both bodies. Mirrors take the modelled width to
# 1.952 m.
#
# Colours: Nightfire Red, Silver Leaf metallic, White Gold and Tahiti Blue, Rover
# UK offerings of 1995-99. DVSA first-registration shares are measured for these
# years, so no era adjustment is needed: red 5, silver 4, white 3, blue 3.
# Total 15. Provisional pending the central rebase.
#
# Speed 180 km/h, a step above the R8 400 for the later engine range. Comfort
# 92/320, on the line 180 + 1.5 x 92, a fraction above the R8's 90/315 for the
# revised trim and sound deadening. Classes 1,2.
#
# Dates are road presence, not production: June 1995 intro, retires staggered
# 2007-2011 on a twelve- to sixteen-year tail.
#
# Metres master: citycars/4.5/rover-400-hhr.blend. Interchange:
# citycars/4.5/rover-400-hhr.dae (69 objects). References:
# citycars/references/rover-400-hhr/ (gitignored; Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0).
#
# Accepted low-poly deviations, recorded rather than corrected: the surfaces are a
# simplified, more angular interpretation of the photographed compound curves, and
# the rear lamps are the taller, narrower units the comparison called for rather
# than the first pass's shape.

obj=citycar
name=Rover400HHR(NightfireRed)
copyright=JamesPetts

DistributionWeight=5
intro_year=1995
intro_month=6
retire_year=2009
retire_month=6
speed=180
class=1,2
maximum_comfortable_journey_minutes=92
maximum_tolerable_journey_minutes=320

